1
00:00:03,128 --> 00:00:05,670
INSTRUCTOR: Hello, everyone,
and welcome to the content video

2
00:00:05,670 --> 00:00:07,350
for Graphs--

3
00:00:07,350 --> 00:00:12,510
A Currency Exchange
Rate Arbitrage Example.

4
00:00:12,510 --> 00:00:16,470
OK, so a great example of
graphs used in the real world

5
00:00:16,470 --> 00:00:19,115
are currencies and
foreign exchange.

6
00:00:19,115 --> 00:00:20,740
So maybe you've heard
of forex trading.

7
00:00:20,740 --> 00:00:22,270
That stands for
foreign exchange.

8
00:00:22,270 --> 00:00:25,090
That's where you trade
currencies between themselves.

9
00:00:25,090 --> 00:00:27,420
So you start with
US dollars traded

10
00:00:27,420 --> 00:00:30,330
for euro, traded to another
currency, come back to us

11
00:00:30,330 --> 00:00:32,710
dollars, things like that.

12
00:00:32,710 --> 00:00:38,650
So a specific strategy in forex
is called triangular arbitrage.

13
00:00:38,650 --> 00:00:40,950
That's where you start
in a certain currency

14
00:00:40,950 --> 00:00:42,810
and then move around
to different currencies

15
00:00:42,810 --> 00:00:44,518
and come back to the
original currencies,

16
00:00:44,518 --> 00:00:46,200
and you've made a slight profit.

17
00:00:46,200 --> 00:00:48,113
In fact, here is an example.

18
00:00:48,113 --> 00:00:49,780
Here's a graph of
what I just mentioned.

19
00:00:49,780 --> 00:00:52,080
So triangular arbitrage--
in this example,

20
00:00:52,080 --> 00:00:56,950
it starts with $5 million in
US with a Citibank account.

21
00:00:56,950 --> 00:01:01,440
Then you monitor exchange rates
and realize, hey, I can go to--

22
00:01:01,440 --> 00:01:05,080
I can go to in euro
in Deutsche Bank

23
00:01:05,080 --> 00:01:11,770
and then go to pound
with Crédit Agricole

24
00:01:11,770 --> 00:01:14,300
and then come back to
US through Barclays.

25
00:01:14,300 --> 00:01:17,240
And those exchange
rates will work out.

26
00:01:17,240 --> 00:01:20,290
So when I come back to my
$5 million at Citibank,

27
00:01:20,290 --> 00:01:27,670
I have $5,025,406, given me an
arbitrage profit of $25,000.

28
00:01:27,670 --> 00:01:29,530
That's a triangular arbitrage.

29
00:01:29,530 --> 00:01:31,520
Well, what is that right there?

30
00:01:31,520 --> 00:01:32,870
Look at that data structure.

31
00:01:32,870 --> 00:01:33,790
That's a graph.

32
00:01:33,790 --> 00:01:36,250
To be specific, that's
a directed graph,

33
00:01:36,250 --> 00:01:38,680
where the nodes are currencies.

34
00:01:38,680 --> 00:01:40,880
And the edges are
exchange rates.

35
00:01:40,880 --> 00:01:42,730
So how could you do
this in real life?

36
00:01:42,730 --> 00:01:44,560
Well, you would need
to get those exchange

37
00:01:44,560 --> 00:01:49,330
rates in real time and then have
a way to take those currencies

38
00:01:49,330 --> 00:01:51,700
and move them from one
currency to another.

39
00:01:51,700 --> 00:01:54,880
If you could do
that and then find

40
00:01:54,880 --> 00:01:57,740
the disequilibrium in real
time, you could trade this.

41
00:01:57,740 --> 00:02:00,550
So when all things
are in equilibrium,

42
00:02:00,550 --> 00:02:02,650
if I started with $5
million, it would come back

43
00:02:02,650 --> 00:02:04,400
and be $5 million again.

44
00:02:04,400 --> 00:02:07,780
But when exchange rates
get into disequilibrium,

45
00:02:07,780 --> 00:02:10,300
then there's
arbitrage opportunity.

46
00:02:10,300 --> 00:02:15,640
OK, so how can you do this
in Python using networkx?

47
00:02:15,640 --> 00:02:19,730
All right, so back
to our Python now.

48
00:02:19,730 --> 00:02:22,648
So I'm using networkx here, and
I'm going to do an example using

49
00:02:22,648 --> 00:02:24,940
static data, where I just
put all of the exchange rates

50
00:02:24,940 --> 00:02:26,150
in a 2D array.

51
00:02:26,150 --> 00:02:28,790
So let's take a look here,
so here's my currencies.

52
00:02:28,790 --> 00:02:32,740
I've got the Polish zloty,
the euro, US dollar,

53
00:02:32,740 --> 00:02:36,363
the Russian ruble, the Indian
rupee, and the Mexican peso.

54
00:02:36,363 --> 00:02:37,780
And here's all the
exchange rates.

55
00:02:37,780 --> 00:02:40,460
And I copied these at one point
in time, so they're all static.

56
00:02:40,460 --> 00:02:41,930
They're not updated dynamically.

57
00:02:41,930 --> 00:02:43,960
I'll show you how to do
that in just a second.

58
00:02:43,960 --> 00:02:45,550
So here's all the
exchange rates,

59
00:02:45,550 --> 00:02:47,000
and here's how you read this.

60
00:02:47,000 --> 00:02:50,650
This first list or this first
row in this 2D list is all

61
00:02:50,650 --> 00:02:53,710
of the Polish exchange rates,
the second row, all of the euro,

62
00:02:53,710 --> 00:02:54,650
and so on.

63
00:02:54,650 --> 00:02:56,890
So if I want to
take one Polish--

64
00:02:56,890 --> 00:02:58,990
here's my row for all
my Polish Zoltys--

65
00:02:58,990 --> 00:03:01,180
and turn it into
a euro, I'm going

66
00:03:01,180 --> 00:03:04,610
to get 0.23 euros when
the exchange is done.

67
00:03:04,610 --> 00:03:06,340
If I want to take
my one Polish zloty

68
00:03:06,340 --> 00:03:09,970
and turn it into us
dollars, I get $0.25 US.

69
00:03:09,970 --> 00:03:13,100
If I want to put it into Russian
rubles, I get more rubles.

70
00:03:13,100 --> 00:03:15,050
I get 16.43 rubles.

71
00:03:15,050 --> 00:03:17,720
That's how the exchange--
the exchange rates work.

72
00:03:17,720 --> 00:03:20,930
But that's a perfect graph
data structure right there.

73
00:03:20,930 --> 00:03:23,350
One node is a currency,
goes to another node.

74
00:03:23,350 --> 00:03:26,620
And the edge in between them
represents the exchange rate.

75
00:03:26,620 --> 00:03:28,990
So how do we load
those up into a graph?

76
00:03:28,990 --> 00:03:31,930
Well, we create a graph
object on line 48.

77
00:03:31,930 --> 00:03:34,087
You can see it's a digraph
or a directed graph,

78
00:03:34,087 --> 00:03:35,920
meaning the edges are
directed from one node

79
00:03:35,920 --> 00:03:38,830
to another, in this case,
from one currency to another.

80
00:03:38,830 --> 00:03:41,480
And then we append all these
edges right here, like this.

81
00:03:41,480 --> 00:03:42,920
So check out line 55.

82
00:03:42,920 --> 00:03:45,920
Remember, I'm adding tuples
to this list of edges.

83
00:03:45,920 --> 00:03:48,730
So each of these has
C1 for our currency

84
00:03:48,730 --> 00:03:52,310
1, C2 for our currency 2,
and then our rate from i,

85
00:03:52,310 --> 00:03:55,182
j-- that gives me the
exchange rate from I to j.

86
00:03:55,182 --> 00:03:56,390
And I print a little message.

87
00:03:56,390 --> 00:03:58,390
I'm adding the edge,
I'm using the function

88
00:03:58,390 --> 00:03:59,800
add_weighted_edges_from.

89
00:03:59,800 --> 00:04:02,300
And that function wants
all of them at once.

90
00:04:02,300 --> 00:04:04,490
That's how you create
the graph from scratch,

91
00:04:04,490 --> 00:04:06,530
and I give it all of
my edges to create it.

92
00:04:06,530 --> 00:04:09,190
And then once I've done
that, my graph is built.

93
00:04:09,190 --> 00:04:11,200
All right, so how do
I traverse it now?

94
00:04:11,200 --> 00:04:15,310
We talked about graph traversal
in the past couple of videos.

95
00:04:15,310 --> 00:04:17,260
So I traverse this
graph, but I'm looking

96
00:04:17,260 --> 00:04:18,529
for something specific here.

97
00:04:18,529 --> 00:04:23,000
I'm looking for disequilibrium
or opportunity for arbitrage.

98
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:25,492
Well, if an exchange rate--

99
00:04:25,492 --> 00:04:26,950
take a look at
these exchange rate.

100
00:04:26,950 --> 00:04:28,930
What do you notice when
they're in equilibrium?

101
00:04:28,930 --> 00:04:30,880
If you multiply
the exchange rate

102
00:04:30,880 --> 00:04:36,010
from the Polish zloty to USD
and USD to the Polish zloty,

103
00:04:36,010 --> 00:04:38,660
if they're in equilibrium,
they should be 1.0.

104
00:04:38,660 --> 00:04:40,600
So let's test that.

105
00:04:40,600 --> 00:04:44,140
0.23 is going from
zloty to US dollar.

106
00:04:44,140 --> 00:04:46,640
And I multiply that times 4.--

107
00:04:46,640 --> 00:04:49,750
what was it again?

108
00:04:49,750 --> 00:04:55,250
4.34, and the answer is 0.9982.

109
00:04:55,250 --> 00:04:57,310
So there's a slight
disequilibrium here.

110
00:04:57,310 --> 00:05:02,370
And again, the reality is,
in real-world exchanges,

111
00:05:02,370 --> 00:05:05,140
there's never perfect,
perfect equilibrium.

112
00:05:05,140 --> 00:05:07,560
So 0.9999 is actually
pretty common.

113
00:05:07,560 --> 00:05:11,580
In fact, 0.9998-- that actually
has some disequilibrium in it.

114
00:05:11,580 --> 00:05:14,880
OK, but my point is, though,
if I take the exchange rate

115
00:05:14,880 --> 00:05:16,800
from one currency to
another and then back

116
00:05:16,800 --> 00:05:18,800
when I multiply them, if
they're in equilibrium,

117
00:05:18,800 --> 00:05:19,770
they should be one.

118
00:05:19,770 --> 00:05:22,560
So when I traverse these here,
take a look what I'm doing,

119
00:05:22,560 --> 00:05:24,490
line 71 here.

120
00:05:24,490 --> 00:05:27,090
I'm using all the
combinations of these nodes--

121
00:05:27,090 --> 00:05:29,950
of these nodes or
of these currencies.

122
00:05:29,950 --> 00:05:33,180
Recall that a combination
gives me all of the sets

123
00:05:33,180 --> 00:05:36,138
but doesn't care
about order, and I

124
00:05:36,138 --> 00:05:38,680
don't either because I'm going
to check the path to and from.

125
00:05:38,680 --> 00:05:40,572
So I don't want
permutation here like I

126
00:05:40,572 --> 00:05:42,030
would if I'm going
to get the data,

127
00:05:42,030 --> 00:05:43,480
I'll show you that in a second.

128
00:05:43,480 --> 00:05:45,563
But because order doesn't
matter, because I just--

129
00:05:45,563 --> 00:05:47,640
I just need a pair in
any order because I'm

130
00:05:47,640 --> 00:05:51,780
going to check the path going to
and the path going from myself.

131
00:05:51,780 --> 00:05:52,880
So there you go.

132
00:05:52,880 --> 00:05:55,150
Now, once I've got that,
I use this really nice,

133
00:05:55,150 --> 00:05:57,020
easy-to-use function called nx.

134
00:05:57,020 --> 00:05:59,780
This is a network
function all_simple_paths.

135
00:05:59,780 --> 00:06:02,710
This gives me a list of all
the paths from one node, n1,

136
00:06:02,710 --> 00:06:04,567
one to another node, n2.

137
00:06:04,567 --> 00:06:07,150
So I'm going to go through all
the combinations of currencies,

138
00:06:07,150 --> 00:06:09,850
all the currency pairs
and check all of the paths

139
00:06:09,850 --> 00:06:11,390
from one currency to another.

140
00:06:11,390 --> 00:06:13,750
Then I'm going to multiply
them, so take a look at lines

141
00:06:13,750 --> 00:06:16,090
81 through 83 here.

142
00:06:16,090 --> 00:06:18,700
I'm going through this
path, and I'm multiplying

143
00:06:18,700 --> 00:06:20,680
all of the edges I see.

144
00:06:20,680 --> 00:06:23,260
Recall from our
previous video, I

145
00:06:23,260 --> 00:06:26,780
can access these edges
dictionary style on my graph.

146
00:06:26,780 --> 00:06:28,720
So g is my graph object.

147
00:06:28,720 --> 00:06:31,150
Path i is the first
node in this edge.

148
00:06:31,150 --> 00:06:32,990
Path i plus 1 is the second one.

149
00:06:32,990 --> 00:06:35,200
And then I use the key
weight to give me the weight.

150
00:06:35,200 --> 00:06:37,325
And then I just go through
and multiply all of them

151
00:06:37,325 --> 00:06:38,150
in that path.

152
00:06:38,150 --> 00:06:40,180
And when I have the path
to and the path from,

153
00:06:40,180 --> 00:06:43,030
if everything's in
equilibrium, it should be 1.0.

154
00:06:43,030 --> 00:06:45,290
So let's take a look here.

155
00:06:45,290 --> 00:06:48,240
OK, let me grab an example here.

156
00:06:48,240 --> 00:06:51,350
Let's go Path To.

157
00:06:51,350 --> 00:06:51,850
Here we go.

158
00:06:51,850 --> 00:06:53,230
Let's do Path.

159
00:06:53,230 --> 00:06:54,910
So I'll just grab one.

160
00:06:54,910 --> 00:07:01,640
From INR to Mexican peso, I
have a path weight of 0.26.

161
00:07:01,640 --> 00:07:05,200
Then going from Mexican peso to
INR, which is an Indian rupee--

162
00:07:05,200 --> 00:07:07,150
it's 3.65.

163
00:07:07,150 --> 00:07:10,810
So let's try this to see, did
we get any disequilibrium here?

164
00:07:10,810 --> 00:07:12,380
So I'm going to copy that.

165
00:07:12,380 --> 00:07:14,628
There's the exchange
rate of that path.

166
00:07:14,628 --> 00:07:15,670
That's not just one edge.

167
00:07:15,670 --> 00:07:19,850
That's the path of
the rupee to peso.

168
00:07:19,850 --> 00:07:23,970
And then I'm going to grab the
path back from peso to rupee.

169
00:07:26,595 --> 00:07:27,680
Oh, hang on.

170
00:07:27,680 --> 00:07:30,850
Mixing up my tabs here.

171
00:07:30,850 --> 00:07:35,200
And it's 0.95, so it
is in disequilibrium.

172
00:07:35,200 --> 00:07:37,713
And then when I go
through and find--

173
00:07:37,713 --> 00:07:39,880
if I go through all of them,
I can figure out, well,

174
00:07:39,880 --> 00:07:43,000
which path is in the
greatest disequilibrium?

175
00:07:43,000 --> 00:07:45,430
And you want it to give you
a number larger than 1.0

176
00:07:45,430 --> 00:07:47,860
because, again, back to
triangular arbitrage,

177
00:07:47,860 --> 00:07:49,450
I want the number
to be greater when

178
00:07:49,450 --> 00:07:51,040
I get back around the cycle.

179
00:07:51,040 --> 00:07:52,957
So the difference between
triangular arbitrage

180
00:07:52,957 --> 00:07:55,040
and what we're doing here
in this Python example--

181
00:07:55,040 --> 00:07:56,810
this has more than
three currencies in it.

182
00:07:56,810 --> 00:07:59,500
This one has, what
was it, seven--

183
00:07:59,500 --> 00:08:00,510
six currencies in it.

184
00:08:00,510 --> 00:08:02,510
But you can have as many
currencies as you want.

185
00:08:02,510 --> 00:08:04,120
So instead of doing
a triangle, we're

186
00:08:04,120 --> 00:08:07,392
doing a full cycle
in a graph, one path

187
00:08:07,392 --> 00:08:09,850
all the way around to another
currency and then all the way

188
00:08:09,850 --> 00:08:12,890
back to look for disequilibrium.

189
00:08:12,890 --> 00:08:14,935
OK, so that's for static data.

190
00:08:14,935 --> 00:08:16,560
How would you do it
for real-time data?

191
00:08:16,560 --> 00:08:18,143
What if you wanted
to do this for real

192
00:08:18,143 --> 00:08:20,540
to see if-- is there
really just equilibrium

193
00:08:20,540 --> 00:08:22,230
in real-time exchange rates?

194
00:08:22,230 --> 00:08:24,170
Well, this example right
here is very similar,

195
00:08:24,170 --> 00:08:26,490
but I don't have static
data in a 2D array.

196
00:08:26,490 --> 00:08:30,485
I actually go and get these
exchange rates from float rates.

197
00:08:30,485 --> 00:08:32,360
So float rates-- let me
go ahead and run this

198
00:08:32,360 --> 00:08:36,100
because it'll give me
the URLs when I run here.

199
00:08:36,100 --> 00:08:38,250
So this example right
here-- let's take a look.

200
00:08:38,250 --> 00:08:41,770
I'm using this URL
float rates, and it's

201
00:08:41,770 --> 00:08:42,909
going through all of them.

202
00:08:42,909 --> 00:08:44,450
I just want to grab one of them.

203
00:08:47,638 --> 00:08:48,930
Let's take a look at this data.

204
00:08:48,930 --> 00:08:52,100
And again, using the Andy
Brim method of processing data

205
00:08:52,100 --> 00:08:56,027
from a web JSON API, put in the
url, hit Enter, stare at it,

206
00:08:56,027 --> 00:08:57,860
figure out the keys you
know, and then write

207
00:08:57,860 --> 00:08:59,140
the code to process it.

208
00:08:59,140 --> 00:09:00,290
So I put in the URL.

209
00:09:00,290 --> 00:09:02,690
I'm going to stare at it,
and I realize, OK, these are

210
00:09:02,690 --> 00:09:05,990
the rates for the Indian rupee.

211
00:09:05,990 --> 00:09:07,650
Here's the US rate.

212
00:09:07,650 --> 00:09:08,880
So check it out right here.

213
00:09:08,880 --> 00:09:12,560
If I want to buy a rupee,
it takes me that much USD,

214
00:09:12,560 --> 00:09:14,850
0.012, right there.

215
00:09:14,850 --> 00:09:16,550
So what I can do
here is I can then

216
00:09:16,550 --> 00:09:19,200
figure out the keys I
need to get these rates.

217
00:09:19,200 --> 00:09:23,480
I need the-- well, if I'm
interested in the Indian rupee

218
00:09:23,480 --> 00:09:28,580
to USD exchange rate, then I
go, I want this URL and then

219
00:09:28,580 --> 00:09:31,950
this key right here, USD, and
then this key rate right here.

220
00:09:31,950 --> 00:09:36,880
So I would go back to my python,
save the USD and the rates.

221
00:09:36,880 --> 00:09:40,950
And I've got those right here.

222
00:09:40,950 --> 00:09:43,380
USD is actually my
second currency.

223
00:09:43,380 --> 00:09:45,770
And then I create
my URL with that--

224
00:09:45,770 --> 00:09:48,250
C1 in that example
would be my rupee.

225
00:09:48,250 --> 00:09:50,430
I go get my JSON
there, and then I

226
00:09:50,430 --> 00:09:54,610
get my rate by my dictionary c2,
which would be USD in my example

227
00:09:54,610 --> 00:09:56,200
and then the rate right there.

228
00:09:56,200 --> 00:09:58,770
So once I've got
my rate, then I can

229
00:09:58,770 --> 00:10:01,870
add my edge c1, c2, and rate.

230
00:10:01,870 --> 00:10:06,700
Now I've got an edge in
that example, rupee to USD.

231
00:10:06,700 --> 00:10:08,153
But I do that for all of them.

232
00:10:08,153 --> 00:10:10,070
And one other-- one other
thing to note here--

233
00:10:10,070 --> 00:10:12,880
In the previous example,
I used combinations

234
00:10:12,880 --> 00:10:16,280
when I wanted to go through all
of the different currency pairs.

235
00:10:16,280 --> 00:10:19,030
But when I specifically
want to go from one currency

236
00:10:19,030 --> 00:10:21,940
to another currency and
the direction matters,

237
00:10:21,940 --> 00:10:24,850
then I want then I
want permutations.

238
00:10:24,850 --> 00:10:27,340
Recall the difference between
permutations and combination.

239
00:10:27,340 --> 00:10:29,330
Permutation order matters.

240
00:10:29,330 --> 00:10:31,288
Like, if you had a
group of 10 people

241
00:10:31,288 --> 00:10:33,580
and you wanted to make a
presidency of three people out

242
00:10:33,580 --> 00:10:35,205
of that, then it
would matter the order

